Wait for the finger before restoring the drawer's position

Nine of the twelve "the app moved a list under a finger" records in one session were
the drawer's own open-restore, moving between one and fifty pixels while the thumb
that had just swiped the drawer open was still resting in it. Opening the drawer is a
swipe, so the hand is routinely still on the screen when the restore runs. It waits
for the last finger to lift now.

The rest of the session's evidence, which is what this round was for:

  * opening a row is confirmed on the device, and by real rows rather than the live
    one: e31083 grew 693px, e31089 423px, e298 271px, e4740 4684px, and the list did
    not move for any of them. The top edge stayed and the row grew downward, which is
    point two of this goal answered where it matters;
  * every movement carries a cause — 11 session-switch, 12 open-restore, 25
    tail-growth, 10 tail-rows, 9 tail-rearm, 2 to-bottom-button;
  * the remaining shifts are content arriving (two rows, kept=50) or the drawer
    re-laying-out its roster while it is shut, which is invisible and is restored on
    open;
  * 9.1% of frames over the 11ms budget in this session against 11.7% before, with
    2.6% over 33ms. The previous session measured 5.4% — the spread is usage, not
    regression.

337 green.